{
  "summary": "Adds framework support for target-specific libFuzzer custom mutators and implements a structure-aware custom mutator for the PSBT deserialization target. The fuzz framework gains an optional mutator callback in FuzzTargetOptions with configure-time detection for engine support and automatic restoration of default length control for targets without custom mutators. The PSBT mutator splits input bytes into global, input, and output key-value maps and mutates keys and values using valid Bitcoin cryptographic formats like secp256k1 pubkeys, xpubs, and Taproot control blocks.",
  "problem": "Raw byte-level fuzzer mutations rarely assemble valid PSBT key-value framing or valid cryptographic structures such as DER signatures and public keys. As a result, the partially_signed_transaction_deserialize target rejects almost all inputs in superficial parsing checks and fails to exercise deeper deserialization logic.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: \"The `partially_signed_transaction_deserialize` target feeds raw bytes to the PSBT parser. Byte-level mutations rarely produce well-formed key-value maps with valid keydata (pubkeys, xpubs, control blocks, DER signatures, etc), so most inputs are rejected early and the deeper per-field parsing is rarely reached.\"",
    "Commit 1d9624833b notes that adding LLVMFuzzerCustomMutator globally disables libFuzzer's -len_control, necessitating logic to restore -len_control=100 for all other targets."
  ],
